About 66 million years ago, an [[asteroid|asteroid]] hit what is now Mexico. Dust and soot darkened the skies, and plants struggled to grow. Most big land animals starved.

Many mammals died too. But the [[@who-survived|survivors]] had useful habits. They were small, could shelter in burrows and ate whatever they found: insects, seeds, roots. Small animals also need less food and breed quickly, so their numbers could bounce back.

{{story:who-survived}}

In the 1870s, new railways crossed the plains, and factories in the eastern USA and Europe found they could turn bison hides into tough leather for machine belts. Professional hunters shot thousands of animals a week, took the skins and left the bodies to rot. Passengers sometimes fired at herds from train windows for fun.

Some army officers and politicians openly welcomed the killing, because without bison, Native nations would be forced onto reservations. By 1889, the naturalist William Hornaday, working for the Smithsonian, counted only about a thousand bison left in North America. Piles of bleached bones were collected from the prairie and shipped east to be ground into [[fertiliser|fertiliser]].

{{story:yellowstone-wolves}}

Nobody knew cells existed until there were [[@microscopes|microscopes]]. In 1665, the English scientist Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork and saw rows of tiny boxes. They reminded him of the small rooms monks lived in, called cells, so that's what he named them.

A few years later, the Dutch shopkeeper Antonie van Leeuwenhoek built tiny but powerful lenses of his own. He was among the first to see living bacteria and blood cells, which he described as "little animals" in his letters.

{{story:microscopes}}

The first umbrellas, thousands of years ago, kept off the sun. In ancient Egypt and Assyria, servants held them over kings. In India, a royal umbrella called a *chhatra* showed who was in charge, and you can see umbrellas carved over the heads of rulers and gods in old temple [[sculpture|sculptures]]. Even today, decorated umbrellas are part of festivals like [[@india-wonder-thrissur-pooram|Thrissur Pooram]] in Kerala.

In China, people made umbrellas of oiled paper or silk stretched over bamboo. The oil made the paper waterproof, so these could be used in the rain as well.

{{story:monsoon}}

Once tied, the cloth is dyed, starting with the lightest colour, often yellow. Some knots are then tied over the yellow areas, and the cloth goes into a darker dye, such as red. Each round of tying and dyeing adds a new colour, always moving from light to dark.

When the cloth is finally dry, the threads are pulled off with a sharp tug. The knots spring open, leaving dots of different colours and a crinkled texture. Many sellers leave the cloth still crinkled, so buyers can see it is real hand-tied work. Dyers say the water around Jamnagar helps make especially bright reds, though nobody has fully explained why.

{{story:ajanta-colours}}

Chikankari has around 30 or more named stitches. *Bakhiya*, shadow work, is sewn on the back of the cloth so it shows as a soft outline from the front. *Phanda* and *murri* are tiny knots shaped like grains of rice or millet. *Jaali* stitches tease apart the threads of the cloth to make little windows, without cutting a single thread.

Some pieces use only one or two stitches. Masterpieces may use dozens. Expert embroiderers can make stitches so tiny and even that the back looks almost as neat as the front. Craftspeople say you can judge a chikan piece by turning it inside out. Thread matters too. Embroiderers often split cotton or silk thread into finer strands, so the stitches lie flat and soft. A thick thread would pucker the delicate cloth.

{{story:block-printing}}

The pointed arch was the first trick. Compared with a round arch, it sends more of the weight down and less of it sideways, so it pushes less on the walls. It could also be made taller or shorter while keeping the same width, which made planning easier.

Next came ribbed vaults: stone ceilings built on a frame of crossing stone ribs, with thinner stone filling the gaps. Last came the flying buttress, an arch outside the building leaning against the upper walls. It catches the outward push and carries it down to a heavy pillar on the ground, much as some [[@bridges|bridges]] carry their load to the riverbank.

{{story:bridges}}

Many famous companies began in tiny spaces. In 1939, Bill Hewlett and David Packard started making electronic equipment in a rented garage in Palo Alto, California, with about $538. That garage is now called the birthplace of [[@silicon-valley|Silicon Valley]]. Decades later, Apple's first computers were put together in the Jobs family home.

In 1981, Narayana Murthy and six colleagues started Infosys in [[@pune|Pune]]. Murthy borrowed ₹10,000 from his wife, [[@sudha-murty|Sudha Murty]], to get going. For years, the company struggled. It later became one of India's biggest software firms and helped make Bengaluru a centre of technology.

{{story:steve-wozniak-garage}}

Dhyan Chand, a soldier in the British Indian Army, is India's greatest hockey player. He scored 14 goals at the 1928 Olympics. His dribbling was so tight that, as the story goes, officials in the Netherlands once broke his stick to check it for a magnet.

In the 1936 Berlin Olympics final, India beat the home team, [[@germany|Germany]], 8–1. Dhyan Chand's birthday, 29 August, is India's National Sports Day. In 2021 the country's highest sports award was renamed the Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna.

{{story:national-symbols}}

In the traditional game, the raider had to chant "kabaddi, kabaddi" over and over on a [[@body-breath-hold|single breath]], called the cant. If the chanting stopped, the raider was out. It was a test of [[@lungs-breathe|lungs]] as well as legs.

Modern professional kabaddi gives each raid a 30-second limit. Raiders still chant, but the clock decides. A raider must also cross the baulk line, a line in the opponents' half, for a raid to count, and can earn a bonus point by reaching a further bonus line.

{{story:muscles}}

In the army, a cross-country race offered extra milk to the top runners. Milkha came sixth and was picked for special training. He practised so hard that he sometimes collapsed or was sick after runs.

He made the Indian team for the 1956 Melbourne Olympics but did not get past the first round. There he met the American champion Charles Jenkins, who shared his training methods. Milkha went home determined to do better.

{{story:partition}}

Kabaddi is a tag game where a raider runs into the other team's half while chanting "kabaddi, kabaddi" without taking a breath. Tag someone and get back safely, and you score. Since 2014, the Pro Kabaddi League has made it one of India's most-watched sports on TV.

In kho-kho, chasers sit in a line facing alternate directions. Only one can chase at a time, and they swap by tapping a teammate's back and shouting "kho". In January 2025, New Delhi hosted the first Kho Kho World Cup, and India won both the men's and women's titles.

{{story:sport-gilli-danda}}

Ancient Egyptian children played with balls made of leather or papyrus, dolls of wood and cloth, and carved animals. One famous wooden toy in the British Museum is a cat, or possibly a lion, with a jaw that opens when you pull a string.

In [[@ancient-greece|ancient Greece]], children played with spinning tops, hoops, rattles and knucklebones, small bones thrown like dice. A painted vase from about 440 BCE shows a boy with a yo-yo. When Greek children grew up, they sometimes dedicated their toys to the gods, as a sign that childhood was over.

{{story:wheel-invented}}

In 2012, scientists in North Carolina in the USA started the Belly Button Biodiversity project. They swabbed the navels of 60 volunteers and grew the [[@gross-body-bacteria|bacteria]] they found. In total they counted about 2,400 kinds. Many had never been named before. One person's navel held [[bacteria|bacteria]] that had only been found in soil in Japan, though he had never been there.

Most of these [[microbe|microbes]] are harmless or helpful. A belly button is warm, slightly damp and rarely scrubbed, so it makes a cosy home. The scientists compared it to a rainforest, with a few common kinds and a long tail of rare ones.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}

For centuries, people blamed malaria on bad air. Its name comes from Italian words meaning exactly that. In 1880, the French army doctor Alphonse Laveran found the real cause in Algeria: a tiny [[parasite|parasite]] living in the blood.

But how did it spread? Ronald Ross, a British army doctor born in India, worked in hot, cramped labs in Secunderabad, near Hyderabad. On 20 August 1897, he found the parasite growing in the stomach of an Anopheles mosquito that had fed on a malaria patient. He later showed the whole cycle in birds. Ross won the Nobel Prize in 1902, and 20 August is now marked as World Mosquito Day.

{{story:monsoon}}

Before anyone can live on BAS, India needs a way to get astronauts there and back. That is the job of [[@gaganyaan|Gaganyaan]], India's crewed spacecraft. ISRO has been testing its crew capsule, escape system and parachutes, and plans uncrewed flights first, including one carrying a humanoid robot called Vyommitra.

In 2025, [[@indian-air-force|Indian Air Force]] pilot Shubhanshu Shukla flew to the International Space Station on a private American mission. He spent about 18 days there and did experiments for ISRO, gaining experience that will feed into Gaganyaan and BAS.

{{story:space-body}}

Akbar saw that the Rajput kings of Rajasthan were too strong to beat forever. So he made alliances. In 1562 he married a Rajput princess from Amber, near today's [[@jaipur|Jaipur]], and her family became powerful nobles. Raja Man Singh of Amber became one of his greatest generals.

Not everyone agreed. The [[@chittorgarh|siege of Chittorgarh]] in 1568 ended with a terrible slaughter. Maharana Pratap of Mewar refused to submit and fought Akbar's army at Haldighati in 1576. He kept resisting for the rest of his life and is a hero in Rajasthan today.

{{story:akbar-birbal}}

In the 1760s, James Hargreaves built the spinning jenny, which let one worker spin several threads at once. In 1769, Richard Arkwright patented a spinning machine driven by water power. Two years later he opened a mill at Cromford, beside a river in Derbyshire. It is often called one of the first modern factories. Workers came at set times when a bell rang, and the machines never stopped.

Then came the improved steam engine. James Watt's design, developed from the 1760s onwards, used coal much more efficiently than older engines. Factories no longer needed to be beside rivers. They could be built anywhere, especially in cities.

{{story:steam-engine}}

In 1739, the Persian ruler Nader Shah invaded Delhi and carried off the Mughals' treasures, including the jewelled Peacock Throne. Some accounts say the diamond was set into the throne itself. A popular story says that when Nader Shah first saw the stone, he cried "Koh-i-noor!", meaning "Mountain of light". The story may not be true, but the name stuck.

After Nader Shah was killed in 1747, the diamond passed to Ahmad Shah Durrani, whose [[empire|empire]] was the forerunner of modern Afghanistan. His family kept it for decades. In 1813, the exiled Afghan ruler Shah Shuja handed it to [[@ranjit-singh|Maharaja Ranjit Singh]] in Lahore, who wore it on his arm.

{{story:golconda}}

According to al-Maqrizi, people living near Giza were making offerings to the Sphinx, hoping it would bring good harvests. In 1378, a Sufi sheikh named Muhammad Sa'im al-Dahr was angered by what he saw as idol worship and damaged the face. Al-Maqrizi also wrote that sand blew over the area afterwards, which locals took as revenge.

This is the best-known explanation, but al-Maqrizi was writing decades after the event. Some historians think the face may have been damaged more than once over the centuries. What's clear from the tool marks is that people did it.

{{story:archaeology}}

Along the highest ridges of the Rockies runs an invisible line called the Continental Divide. Rain or snow falling to the west of it flows towards the Pacific. Rain falling to the east flows to the Atlantic, mostly by way of the [[@mississippi|Mississippi]] and the Gulf of Mexico.

Some of North America's big rivers start in the Rockies, including the Colorado, the Rio Grande and the Missouri. Snow piles up on the peaks in winter, then melts slowly through spring and summer. Cities and farms far away, in dry states such as [[@grand-canyon-state|Arizona]] and California, depend on that slow melt for their water.

{{story:glaciers}}

In 1884, delegates from 25 countries met in Washington, DC, for the International Meridian Conference. They chose the [[meridian|meridian]], or north-south line, running through the Royal Observatory at Greenwich, in London, as the starting line for longitude and world time.

Today world time is called Coordinated Universal Time, or UTC. Time zones are described as hours ahead of or behind it. New York is 5 hours behind in winter, and [[@tokyo|Tokyo]] is 9 hours ahead.

{{story:day-night}}

All that spray creates its own weather. On sunny days, rainbows hang in the gorge, sometimes double ones. Around the full moon, the spray can even make a moonbow, a faint rainbow by moonlight.

{{story:rainbow-how}}

The constant drizzle feeds a small strip of rainforest on the Zimbabwe side, with ferns, palms and fig trees, in a region that is otherwise dry [[savanna|savanna]]. Monkeys, baboons and birds live in it. Just upstream, hippos and crocodiles live in the river, and elephants sometimes wander along the banks.

In Jiangyong County in Hunan province, China, women once wrote in a script called *Nüshu*, which means "women's writing". For centuries, most girls there were not taught to read Chinese characters. So women developed their own slender, slanted script to write songs, letters and diaries.

Mothers taught it to daughters, and friends sewed it into fans and handkerchiefs. When a girl married and moved away, her friends sometimes gave her a book of Nüshu songs. Many writings were burnt or buried with their owners, so fewer survive than were written. The last woman who learnt it the traditional way died in 2004. Today, it is taught again, and China has listed it as part of its national heritage.

{{story:code-talkers}}

The yolk is the chick's food store. It holds fat, protein, iron and [[vitamin|vitamins]]. On its surface is a tiny pale spot called the germinal disc. In a fertilised egg, that's where the chick starts to form.

The white cushions the yolk and holds extra water and protein. It also contains natural substances that fight [[bacteria|bacteria]]. When you crack an egg, you can see that the white comes in two parts: a thick layer around the yolk and a runnier layer outside.

{{story:dino-eggs}}

For most of the 20th century, many Western scientists did not accept umami as a basic taste. They thought it was just a mix of salty and other flavours.

The proof came from the tongue itself. Around 2000 to 2002, researchers found taste [[receptor|receptors]], tiny protein sensors on taste cells, that respond to glutamate and almost nothing else. A taste with its own receptor counts as a basic taste. Umami was in.

{{story:japan-culture}}
